Interoperability

bfinger speaks published standards and holds no private protocol. Any
wallet that implements BRC-100 can hold the key it signs with; any
overlay host that implements BRC-22 and BRC-24 can carry the objects and
answer for them; any domain that publishes a BRC-180 manifest and a
BRC-169 resolve endpoint can publish the address mapping. Nothing here
requires a particular network operator, and a record published through
this tool remains readable by anything that can read the chain and reach
a host that holds it.

